0 Kiddicraft Self-Locking Building Brick, 2 x 2 - the second brick (A3-S03)
0 Name: kiddicraft.ldr
0 Author: spex project (hand-authored placement data; not sourced from an official ldraw.org models/ release)
0 // Places `parts/brick-2x2-tubeless.dat`, which sits beside this file and is
0 // NOT an official LDraw part. See that file's own header for the whole
0 // derivation: 3003.dat and its two subparts, flattened, with the single
0 // interior `stud4.dat` line removed.
0 //
0 // The tube inside a brick is the 1958 patent. A Kiddicraft Self-Locking
0 // brick and a 1949 Automatic Binding Brick are hollow underneath - the grip
0 // came from the studs against the walls, which is why they held so badly.
0 // Building these three out of a modern 3001/3003 would put a 1958 feature
0 // under a pre-1958 caption, which is exactly the claim this act makes.
0 //
0 // Colour 15 for all three: they ARE the same object, and that is the whole
0 // content of Interlego v Tyco. Three colours would be an editorial claim
0 // the act spends three shots arguing against.
0 //
0 // 2 x 2, because that is the classic Kiddicraft size. The Brighton Toy
0 // Museum shows both 2 x 2 and 2 x 4, so size is not what identifies them -
0 // the end slots are, and those are still missing (noted in the part file).
0 //
0 // Brick height 24 LDU, origin at the footprint centre, LDraw +Y down: a
0 // brick standing on the ground plane is translated to y = -24.
1 15 20120 -24 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 brick-2x2-tubeless.dat
